# FloodWatch Manouba

Prototype flood-risk web map for the Manouba governorate (Greater Tunis, Tunisia): where water naturally collects, combined with the current rainfall situation.

Live site: https://zouaghi11.github.io/manouba_flood_monitor/

This is an academic prototype. It is **not** an official warning system. Official warnings come from the national meteorological institute and civil protection.

Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript, no build step. Leaflet 1.9.4 is included in `lib/leaflet` (BSD-2 licence).

## Pages
- **index.html** - susceptibility map, current alert level, short "how to read this" text.
- **citizen.html** - click the map (or use your location) to read the susceptibility of a place, see the current alert, send a flood report.
- **municipality.html** - alert level and the rainfall windows behind it, hourly rain chart, KPIs and exposure table, hospitals and schools to check first, event log, citizen reports (CSV), model card, and a replay of the September 2020 storm with a time slider.
- English / French switch on every page (strings in `js/i18n.js`).

## Two susceptibility maps
A switch on the map selects the map. The choice is remembered and applies to the legend, popups, KPIs, exposure table and facility markers.

| Map | How it is made | Held-out AUC | Radar flood pixels in the riskiest 20% of land |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Evidence-based** (default) | Logistic regression on terrain height above nearest drainage (HAND) and slope, fitted on Sentinel-1 flood pixels | 0.86 | 77% |
| **Expert (AHP)** | Weighted overlay of HAND, flow accumulation, land cover and slope with AHP weights (CR 0.012) | 0.66 | 34% |

Both are tested on the same 4,780 radar flood pixels (September 2020 and January 2026) on flat land away from permanent water. Chance level is AUC 0.5 and 20%.

Read the limits below before using either map: radar only sees flooding of open, flat land, so the evidence-based map puts almost all buildings in "medium" (237 buildings in "high", against 4,632 for the AHP map). Neither map has been checked against street-level flooding yet.

## Citizen reports
- Without configuration, reports stay in the visitor's browser (localStorage).
- With a Supabase project, reports are shared: set `reports: { url, key }` in `js/config.js` (publishable / anon key only, never the service_role key) and run `supabase_setup.sql` once in the Supabase SQL editor.
- The public site can only add reports and read a reduced view: last 72 hours, place rounded to about 110 m, no free-text note. The full table is visible only in the Supabase dashboard.
- Reports are unverified and nobody answers them. The citizen page says so and shows the emergency numbers. A real service needs staff login and moderation.
- A database rule limits inserts to 30 per minute in total, and each browser to one report per minute.

## Alert logic (`js/common.js`, settings in `js/config.js`)
Rainfall comes from the Open-Meteo API (hourly precipitation, last 2 days and forecast; archive for the replay). For windows of 1, 3, 6 and 24 hours, past and forecast, the rain total is compared with the **Watch** and **Alert** thresholds. The highest level wins. If data are missing or older than 3 hours the level is **Status unknown**, never "no risk".

Thresholds (mm) come from the Open-Meteo archive, 1991-2025, at 36.81 N 10.09 E: Watch = median and Alert = 90th percentile of the yearly maxima.

| Window | 1 h | 3 h | 6 h | 24 h |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Watch | 7.1 | 13.5 | 17.4 | 30.1 |
| Alert | 9.8 | 19.8 | 28.4 | 47.3 |

The archive is a coarse reanalysis that underestimates local convective storms (the September 2020 storm reaches only Watch in the replay). Check the thresholds against rain gauge records before any real use.

## Data files (`data/`)
| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `risk_eb.png`, `risk_ahp.png` | Colour overlays of the two maps (same grid, EPSG:3857) |
| `flood_s1_overlay.png` | Sentinel-1 flood pixels on land, 15 Sep 2020 (indicative) |
| `boundary.geojson` | Manouba boundary |
| `meta.json` | Overlay bounds and palette, areas and OpenStreetMap exposure per map, hospital and school sites, validation numbers |

All of them are written by the QGIS export script. Do not edit `meta.json` by hand.

## Rebuilding the data
Run these QGIS Python scripts (Run Script in the editor), in this order. The scripts use local paths under `C:/Flood Watch/`.
1. `s1_flood_events.py` - Sentinel-1 flood masks and validation numbers.
2. `build_evidence_map.py` - evidence-based map (`flood_risk_class_eb.tif`).
3. `exposure_osm_overpass_v2.py` - OpenStreetMap exposure for both maps. Objects of the same kind within 100 m are merged into one hospital or school site.
4. `export_web_layers_v2.py` - writes the five data files to `C:/Flood Watch/web_export/`. Copy them into `data/`.

Test locally with a web server (opening the html file directly breaks click-to-read): run `python -m http.server 8000` in this folder and open http://localhost:8000.

## Deployment
GitHub Pages: Settings > Pages > Deploy from a branch > `main` / root. After pushing, force-refresh the browser (Ctrl+F5) to avoid cached scripts.

## Credits and terms
- Weather data by [Open-Meteo.com](https://open-meteo.com/), CC BY 4.0. The public API is for non-commercial use with a daily call limit; an operational service needs a commercial plan or self-hosting.
- Map data © [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright) contributors. The standard tile server allows light use only; use another tile provider for more traffic.
- Land cover © ESA WorldCover. Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data from the Copernicus programme.
- Rainfall check: NASA GPM IMERG.

## Limits
- Both maps rank susceptibility. They are not flood forecasts and do not give water depth.
- Validation uses radar flood pixels only: open, flat land, 2 storms (Sept 2020, Jan 2026). Flash flooding in streets and built-up areas is not validated. Radar passes in Oct 2024, Jan 2025 and Sept 2026 gave no usable land signal.
- Rainfall is a model value at one point. Thresholds are not checked against rain gauges.
- OpenStreetMap counts are minimums: completeness is unknown.
- No staff login, no moderation, no SMS or push alerts.
